  <h2>Read the story</h2>

  <h3>The smoke</h3>
  <p>
    Ryu smelled the smoke before he saw it. It came over the bamboo
    forest on the evening wind — wood smoke, and underneath it, the
    sharper smell of burning thatch. He had walked this road a hundred
    times as a boy. He knew what a cooking fire smelled like. This was
    not a cooking fire.
  </p>
  <p>
    He ran. The bamboo blurred past him, green and gold in the sunset,
    and then the trees opened and there it was: Kaze village, burning
    for the second time in three years.
  </p>

  <h3>The vow</h3>
  <p>
    Three years ago, Ryu had buried his katana under the old temple
    floor and walked away from the sword. His master, Hayato, was dead
    — killed by his own student. Ryu had sworn, over the grave, that
    he would never use the fire again.
  </p>
  <p>
    The fire was not a gift. It was a price. Every time the blade
    burned, it burned a part of the man who held it. Once a day, and
    only once — and every use took something he would never get back.
    So he became a farmer. He grew rice. He let the village forget
    the swordsman.
  </p>
  <p>
    The village had not forgotten. The fire had come back for it
    anyway.
  </p>

  <h3>Aiko</h3>
  <p>
    He found her in the old temple, where the smoke was thinnest,
    crouched behind the broken altar with his katana in her hands.
    Aiko, seventeen, his adopted younger sister, her face streaked
    with soot.
  </p>
  <p>
    “You buried it for a reason, Ryu,” she said. “Don't pick it up.”
  </p>
  <p>
    He took the sword from her anyway — not to draw it, just to hold
    it. The red handle was warm from her hands. Outside, the roof of
    the granary collapsed in a shower of sparks.
  </p>

  <h3>Kaito arrives</h3>
  <p>
    The soldiers came at dusk, in perfect order, the way only Kaito's
    men marched. And then Kaito himself, down from his horse, black
    armor drinking the firelight, his ice-blue blade still sheathed.
    He did not come for gold. He did not come for rice. He came for
    the sword — and for the man who had buried it.
  </p>
  <p>
    “Your master died begging, Ryu,” Kaito said, almost gently.
    “Just like your village will.”
  </p>

  <h3>The fight without fire</h3>
  <p>
    Ryu fought them with a plain blade and no fire at all — six
    soldiers, one after another, in the burning street. He was fast,
    and he was angry, and for a while that was enough. He was
    bleeding by the third man, and breathing hard by the fifth.
    Kaito had not even drawn his sword.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then the soldiers took Aiko. They held her at the edge of the
    fire, where the heat made the air shimmer, and Kaito finally
    smiled.
  </p>
  <p>
    “Pick it up,” he said, “or she burns with the village.”
  </p>

  <h3>The cliffhanger</h3>
  <p>
    Ryu's hand closed on the red handle. For the first time in three
    years, the blade remembered him — and the fire woke, red and
    hungry, along its edge.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the dark, Kaito laughed softly. “Finally,” he said.
    “The fire remembers you.”
  </p>
